1
00:00:00,174 --> 00:00:00,920
T.A.I.

2
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:02,220
is becoming a scientist.

3
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:04,080
And that's changing everything.

4
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:12,900
What if the next major scientific breakthrough does not come from a human scientist sitting in a laboratory, studying equations, testing chemicals, or examining a microscope?

5
00:00:13,710 --> 00:00:25,620
What if it comes from a machine that can analyze millions of scientific papers, identify patterns no human has noticed, develop a new hypothesis, design an experiment, and then use robotic equipment to test whether its discovery is actually real?

6
00:00:26,414 --> 00:00:30,980
For centuries, scientific progress depended on human curiosity, patience, and experimentation.

7
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:33,920
Scientists spent years studying complicated problems.

8
00:00:34,670 --> 00:00:39,660
They conducted thousands of experiments, made mistakes, collected data, and slowly moved closer to answers.

9
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:42,380
A discovery that took decades could change the world.

10
00:00:42,760 --> 00:00:44,600
A new medicine could save millions of lives.

11
00:00:45,390 --> 00:00:47,680
A new material could transform entire industries.

12
00:00:48,334 --> 00:00:52,360
And a single breakthrough in physics could completely change how humanity understands the universe.

13
00:00:53,134 --> 00:00:55,320
But today, something extraordinary is happening.

14
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:59,800
Artificial intelligence is beginning to participate in the scientific process itself.

15
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:03,600
It is no longer limited to answering questions or summarizing information.

16
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:15,100
Modern AI systems can help researchers analyze biological structures, predict chemical reactions, design new materials, explore mathematical problems, and even control automated laboratory equipment.

17
00:01:15,694 --> 00:01:17,860
Some systems can generate scientific hypotheses.

18
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:21,060
Others can plan experiments and evaluate the results.

19
00:01:21,774 --> 00:01:30,720
And in a growing number of research environments, artificial intelligence is being connected directly to robotic laboratories that can perform physical experiments with very little human intervention.

20
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:34,400
Imagine a laboratory where an AI system works through the night.

21
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:46,780
It studies previous research, identifies an unanswered question, selects the most promising experiment, instructs robotic equipment to prepare samples, analyzes the results, and uses what it learns to design the next experiment.

22
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:49,860
No scientist needs to stand beside every machine.

23
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:52,840
No researcher needs to manually repeat every step.

24
00:01:53,262 --> 00:01:58,000
The system can continue learning from each experiment and gradually improve its understanding of the problem.

25
00:01:58,702 --> 00:02:01,720
This is the beginning of a completely different way of doing science.

26
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:06,980
And the biggest question is no longer whether artificial intelligence can help scientists work faster.

27
00:02:07,630 --> 00:02:11,980
The question is whether machines could eventually discover things that humans would never have found on their own.

28
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:17,820
To understand why this matters, we first need to understand one of the biggest limitations of traditional scientific research.

29
00:02:18,510 --> 00:02:21,800
The world contains far more information than any human mind can examine.

30
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:23,200
Consider biology.

31
00:02:23,822 --> 00:02:29,120
The human body contains an enormous number of interacting molecules, proteins, genes, and chemical processes.

32
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:32,460
Every disease involves complicated biological mechanisms.

33
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:40,060
A single protein may interact with multiple other molecules, change its shape, perform different functions, and influence entire cellular systems.

34
00:02:40,740 --> 00:02:47,520
For decades, scientists have studied these relationships using experiments, microscopes, computer simulations, and mathematical models.

35
00:02:48,174 --> 00:02:50,320
But the number of possible interactions is enormous.

36
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:56,340
A researcher might spend years investigating a single protein or testing a limited number of chemical compounds.

37
00:02:56,920 --> 00:03:00,760
Even a large scientific team can only explore a tiny portion of the possibilities.

38
00:03:01,480 --> 00:03:03,720
Artificial intelligence approaches this problem differently.

39
00:03:04,260 --> 00:03:11,700
A machine learning system can analyze enormous datasets and search for relationships across information that would be extremely difficult for humans to examine manually.

40
00:03:12,430 --> 00:03:20,820
It can compare molecular structures, identify patterns in biological data, examine previous experiments and predict which possibilities deserve further investigation.

41
00:03:21,710 --> 00:03:25,140
This does not mean the machine automatically understands everything it analyzes.

42
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:31,340
AI predictions can be incorrect, and scientific conclusions still require evidence, but it changes the scale of the search.

43
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:39,200
Instead of examining a small number of possibilities, researchers can use AI to identify promising candidates for millions of potential options.

44
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:46,520
And one of the most important examples of this transformation comes from a problem that challenged biology for decades—the mystery of protein folding.

45
00:03:47,214 --> 00:03:51,020
Proteins are essential molecules that perform countless functions inside living organisms.

46
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:58,080
They help build tissues, transport substances, control chemical reactions, and support the biological processes that keep cells alive.

47
00:03:58,702 --> 00:04:01,200
But a protein is not simply a straight chain of molecules.

48
00:04:01,838 --> 00:04:07,100
It folds into a complicated three-dimensional structure, and that structure often determines what the protein can do.

49
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:14,460
Understanding the shape of a protein can help scientists investigate diseases, understand biological processes, and develop potential medicines.

50
00:04:15,182 --> 00:04:20,260
The problem is that predicting these structures from their underlying amino acid sequences was extraordinarily difficult.

51
00:04:20,820 --> 00:04:23,060
Scientists had spent decades studying protein folding.

52
00:04:23,920 --> 00:04:33,860
Experimental techniques such as X-ray crystallography and cryo-electron microscopy provided powerful ways to examine molecular structures, but they could be expensive, technically challenging, and time-consuming.

53
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:35,360
Then came AlphaFold.

54
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:42,720
Developed by Google DeepMind, AlphaFold used artificial intelligence to predict the three-dimensional structures of proteins with remarkable accuracy.

55
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:45,640
The achievement changed the landscape of structural biology.

56
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:55,860
The AlphaFold protein structure database eventually made predictions available for more than 200 million protein structures, giving researchers around the world access to an enormous resource.

57
00:04:56,718 --> 00:05:07,320
The technology became so influential that its developers Demis Hesavas and John Jumper received the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry shared with David Baker for his work in computational protein design.

58
00:05:07,886 --> 00:05:09,180
But the story did not end there.

59
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:20,760
AlphaFold3 expanded the technology beyond individual protein structures, helping researchers predict how proteins interact with other biological molecules, including DNA, RNA, and small chemical compounds.

60
00:05:21,454 --> 00:05:22,400
Think about the implications.

61
00:05:23,214 --> 00:05:28,920
A scientist investigating a disease can use AI-powered tools to examine proteins involved in the biological process.

62
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:34,760
Researchers can investigate possible molecular interactions and identify promising directions for further experiments.

63
00:05:35,534 --> 00:05:38,220
This does not instantly create a medicine or cure a disease.

64
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:42,820
Predicting how molecules interact is only one part of an extremely complicated process.

65
00:05:43,740 --> 00:05:49,360
A potential treatment must still be tested in laboratories, evaluated for safety, and studied through clinical trials.

66
00:05:50,126 --> 00:05:55,500
But AI is helping scientists investigate biological questions at a scale that was previously much harder to achieve.

67
00:05:56,060 --> 00:05:57,820
And protein folding is only the beginning.

68
00:05:58,240 --> 00:06:00,120
The next major frontier is drug discovery.

69
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:05,100
Developing a new medicine is one of the most expensive and complicated processes in modern science.

70
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:13,900
Researchers must understand a disease, identify a biological target, find a molecule that could influence that target, and then test whether the molecule works safely.

71
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:16,520
Traditional drug discovery can take many years.

72
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:21,320
Scientists may investigate thousands of chemical compounds before finding a promising candidate.

73
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:24,320
Artificial intelligence is beginning to change this process.

74
00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:31,160
Companies such as Insilico Medicine are using AI to identify potential disease targets and design new drug molecules.

75
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:38,580
One particularly interesting example involves idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, a serious disease that causes progressive scarring of lung tissue.

76
00:06:39,340 --> 00:06:47,220
Researchers at Insilico used AI to help identify a previously underexplored biological target and design a potential drug candidate called rentosartib.

77
00:06:47,790 --> 00:06:52,160
The candidate progressed into human clinical testing, including a published Phase IIa study.

78
00:06:52,910 --> 00:06:57,120
This is important because it demonstrates how AI can contribute to more than theoretical predictions.

79
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:02,780
It can help move scientific discoveries toward real experimental testing, however, there is a critical distinction.

80
00:07:03,380 --> 00:07:05,980
An AI-designed drug is not automatically a successful medicine.

81
00:07:06,766 --> 00:07:11,900
It must still pass rigorous clinical trials, demonstrate safety, and prove that its benefits outweigh its risks.

82
00:07:12,334 --> 00:07:13,620
That process can take years.

83
00:07:14,414 --> 00:07:22,560
Still, the ability to use AI to explore biological targets and design potential molecules could help researchers investigate possibilities that might otherwise remain undiscovered.

84
00:07:23,246 --> 00:07:26,880
And pharmaceutical research is not the only field experiencing this transformation.

85
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:31,160
Scientists are also using artificial intelligence to search for new materials.

86
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:39,920
Imagine trying to develop a material that is lighter than steel, stronger than conventional alloys, more resistant to heat, and capable of surviving extreme industrial conditions.

87
00:07:40,590 --> 00:07:44,220
Finding such a material is not simply a matter of mixing random chemicals together.

88
00:07:44,686 --> 00:07:51,160
Its properties depend on atomic structures, chemical compositions, manufacturing conditions, and interactions between different elements.

89
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:56,240
A scientist may need to examine thousands of possible combinations before finding something useful.

90
00:07:56,680 --> 00:07:58,140
AI can help narrow that search.

91
00:07:58,920 --> 00:08:06,640
Machine learning models can predict the properties of hypothetical materials, estimate their stability, and identify promising structures for further investigation.

92
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:12,240
Researchers can then use computer simulations and physical experiments to test the most interesting candidates.

93
00:08:12,720 --> 00:08:14,680
One important area is battery technology.

94
00:08:15,380 --> 00:08:19,080
The future of electric vehicles and renewable energy depends heavily on better batteries.

95
00:08:19,566 --> 00:08:25,220
Scientists are searching for materials that can store more energy, charge faster, last longer, and operate more safely.

96
00:08:26,080 --> 00:08:30,820
AI can help investigate new battery chemistries and identify materials that may improve performance.

97
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:37,760
It can also support research into catalysts, semiconductors, superconductors, and materials capable of surviving extreme temperatures.

98
00:08:38,574 --> 00:08:41,160
This could have consequences far beyond consumer electronics.

99
00:08:41,980 --> 00:08:49,780
Better materials could improve electric vehicles, aerospace engineering, industrial manufacturing, energy storage, and the infrastructure powering the digital world.

100
00:08:50,382 --> 00:08:53,920
But there is another development that makes this entire revolution even more interesting.

101
00:08:54,660 --> 00:08:58,300
Scientists are beginning to connect AI systems directly to physical laboratories.

102
00:08:59,118 --> 00:09:02,740
For a long time, artificial intelligence operated mainly inside computers.

103
00:09:03,340 --> 00:09:06,620
It could analyze data, generate predictions, and suggest experiments.

104
00:09:07,118 --> 00:09:09,220
But humans still had to perform the physical work.

105
00:09:09,806 --> 00:09:15,580
Now researchers are combining AI with robotic equipment, automated instruments, and laboratory control systems.

106
00:09:15,982 --> 00:09:18,540
These environments are often called self-driving laboratories.

107
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:27,540
Imagine a research facility filled with robotic arms, chemical containers, automated pipettes, microscopes, testing equipment, and computer-controlled instruments.

108
00:09:28,380 --> 00:09:34,900
Instead of a human researcher manually carrying out every procedure, an AI system can help decide which experiment should happen next.

109
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:37,840
The system sends instructions to laboratory equipment.

110
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:39,540
Robots prepare samples.

111
00:09:40,320 --> 00:09:41,480
Instruments collect measurements.

112
00:09:42,062 --> 00:09:44,040
The resulting data returns to the AI model.

113
00:09:44,654 --> 00:09:48,940
The AI examines the outcome and uses the information to decide what should be tested next.

114
00:09:49,550 --> 00:09:51,860
This creates a continuous scientific feedback loop.

115
00:09:52,420 --> 00:09:53,780
A prediction leads to an experiment.

116
00:09:54,220 --> 00:09:55,600
The experiment produces evidence.

117
00:09:55,880 --> 00:09:57,480
The evidence improves the next prediction.

118
00:09:57,902 --> 00:09:58,900
And the cycle continues.

119
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:05,420
A particularly interesting development emerged from Chalmers University of Technology in Sweden in September 2026.

120
00:10:06,286 --> 00:10:15,220
Researchers developed an AI scientist capable of generating biological hypotheses, processes, recommending experiments, and evaluating experimental outcomes in research involving brewer's yeast.

121
00:10:15,822 --> 00:10:22,000
The system combined large language models, scientific knowledge, automated reasoning, and laboratory experimentation.

122
00:10:22,958 --> 00:10:30,532
Rather than simply answering questions about biology, it could help identify promising research questions and refine its understanding based on experimental results.

123
00:10:31,300 --> 00:10:36,680
This is an important step because it demonstrates how AI can participate in multiple stages of the scientific process.

124
00:10:36,680 --> 00:10:39,600
The system was not simply reading scientific papers.

125
00:10:40,078 --> 00:10:42,520
It was helping generate and test scientific ideas.

126
00:10:43,150 --> 00:10:45,360
And the development of these systems is moving rapidly.

127
00:10:46,020 --> 00:10:53,940
In another important development, a research team reported an AI-native autonomous biomolecular laboratory called AutoDNA in November 2026.

128
00:10:54,510 --> 00:11:00,320
The system was designed to coordinate more than 20 types of laboratory instruments across more than 11,000 experimental steps.

129
00:11:01,038 --> 00:11:07,560
It demonstrated automated biological operations involving DNA synthesis, transcription, amplification, and sequencing.

130
00:11:08,430 --> 00:11:16,780
The researchers also reported applications involving DNA data storage, and the identification and validation of a clinically detected cancer-associated mutation.

131
00:11:17,454 --> 00:11:21,580
This is a remarkable demonstration of how AI and laboratory automation can work together.

132
00:11:22,158 --> 00:11:25,440
The important point is not that human scientists have suddenly become unnecessary.

133
00:11:25,454 --> 00:11:25,720
necessary.

134
00:11:26,220 --> 00:11:31,740
It is that complex experimental workflows can increasingly be coordinated by intelligent software and automated equipment.

135
00:11:32,600 --> 00:11:36,220
A laboratory can become something closer to a connected scientific production system.

136
00:11:37,006 --> 00:11:44,620
And once this technology becomes more reliable, the possibilities could expand into chemistry, biotechnology, medicine, and materials science.

137
00:11:44,942 --> 00:11:49,220
But there is another field where AI's involvement may become even more surprising – mathematics.

138
00:11:50,062 --> 00:11:52,740
Mathematics is often described as the language of science.

139
00:11:53,166 --> 00:11:58,480
It allows researchers to describe physical laws, model complex systems, and make predictions about the universe.

140
00:11:58,990 --> 00:12:01,200
Yet many mathematical problems remain unsolved.

141
00:12:01,840 --> 00:12:03,680
Some have challenged researchers for generations.

142
00:12:04,420 --> 00:12:11,800
Modern AI systems are becoming increasingly capable of assisting with mathematical reasoning, exploring possible solutions, and working with formal proof systems.

143
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:18,000
Unlike an ordinary chatbot that generates an explanation, a mathematical proof must follow strict logical rules.

144
00:12:18,420 --> 00:12:19,960
A convincing answer is not enough.

145
00:12:20,270 --> 00:12:21,880
every important step must be justified.

146
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:24,580
This is why formal verification is so important.

147
00:12:25,440 --> 00:12:29,820
It allows a proof to be checked by a computer system against precisely defined mathematical rules.

148
00:12:30,620 --> 00:12:37,020
AI systems can help researchers explore possible approaches, generate candidate proofs, and work through complicated logical problems.

149
00:12:37,760 --> 00:12:41,380
Researchers are also investigating how AI can contribute to physics and engineering.

150
00:12:42,080 --> 00:12:48,940
In physics, machine learning models can help analyze enormous datasets from experiments, identify unusual patterns, and accelerate simulations.

151
00:12:48,940 --> 00:12:49,160
observations.

152
00:12:49,742 --> 00:12:59,000
In astronomy, AI can examine telescope observations and help researchers identify distant galaxies, unusual cosmic objects, and potential signals hidden inside enormous amounts of data.

153
00:12:59,680 --> 00:13:05,940
In climate science, AI can assist with weather prediction, environmental monitoring, and modeling complicated atmospheric systems.

154
00:13:06,510 --> 00:13:07,740
The common idea is simple.

155
00:13:08,600 --> 00:13:15,380
Scientific research produces enormous amounts of information, and artificial intelligence can help researchers find meaningful patterns inside it.

156
00:13:16,046 --> 00:13:18,080
But this is where the story becomes more complicated.

157
00:13:18,862 --> 00:13:21,800
Because finding a pattern is not the same as discovering the truth.

158
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:26,460
An AI system can produce an answer that sounds convincing but is scientifically incorrect.

159
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:34,320
It can misunderstand a research paper, invent a reference, confuse correlation with causation, or recommend an experiment based on flawed assumptions.

160
00:13:35,054 --> 00:13:40,960
And when an AI system controls laboratory equipment, a mistake can become more than an incorrect sentence on a computer screen.

161
00:13:40,960 --> 00:13:45,080
It could waste expensive materials, damage equipment, or create a safety risk.

162
00:13:45,660 --> 00:13:51,740
This is why scientists cannot simply hand over the keys to a laboratory and assume that artificial intelligence will always make the right decisions.

163
00:13:52,380 --> 00:13:54,980
Every scientific discovery must be evaluated through evidence.

164
00:13:55,680 --> 00:13:57,080
An experiment must be repeatable.

165
00:13:57,500 --> 00:13:58,660
Measurements must be reliable.

166
00:13:59,180 --> 00:14:01,140
Results must survive independent investigation.

167
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:04,320
A machine-generated hypothesis is only a starting point.

168
00:14:04,860 --> 00:14:08,100
Even a prediction that looks extraordinary must be tested in the real world.

169
00:14:08,460 --> 00:14:10,060
There is also the problem of understanding.

170
00:14:10,600 --> 00:14:15,100
What happens when an AI system discovers a relationship that works but cannot explain why it works?

171
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:19,080
Imagine a machine identifying a new material with remarkable properties.

172
00:14:19,740 --> 00:14:26,440
Scientists reproduce the results and confirm that the material behaves exactly as predicted, but the underlying mechanism remains unclear.

173
00:14:27,320 --> 00:14:33,480
Has the machine discovered something genuinely new about nature, or has it found a complicated statistical relationship that humans have not yet understood?

174
00:14:34,254 --> 00:14:35,280
This is a difficult question.

175
00:14:35,800 --> 00:14:37,800
Science is not simply about producing useful answers.

176
00:14:38,670 --> 00:14:41,380
It is also about understanding why those answers are correct.

177
00:14:42,190 --> 00:14:48,000
For this reason, the future of AI-driven science may depend on a partnership between machine intelligence and human judgment.

178
00:14:48,880 --> 00:14:54,440
Machines can search enormous spaces, identify patterns, and perform repetitive calculations at incredible speed.

179
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:01,080
Humans can provide scientific context, question assumptions, interpret unexpected results, and decide which discoveries matter.

180
00:15:01,780 --> 00:15:04,640
Together, they could become more effective than either working alone.

181
00:15:04,640 --> 00:15:08,300
But this partnership may also change what it means to be a scientist.

182
00:15:08,920 --> 00:15:20,480
Instead of spending most of their time manually searching databases, processing measurements, and repeating routine experiments, researchers could increasingly focus on designing scientific questions, evaluating evidence, and guiding intelligent systems.

183
00:15:21,326 --> 00:15:26,460
A single research team might coordinate multiple AI agents, each investigating a different aspect of the same problem.

184
00:15:27,214 --> 00:15:29,460
One system could examine previous scientific literature.

185
00:15:29,900 --> 00:15:31,680
Another could model molecular interactions.

186
00:15:31,680 --> 00:15:34,460
A third could design potential experiments.

187
00:15:35,120 --> 00:15:38,200
And an automated laboratory could test the most promising results.

188
00:15:38,780 --> 00:15:42,780
The human scientist would become the person directing and validating the overall process.

189
00:15:43,470 --> 00:15:45,720
This could create an entirely new research environment.

190
00:15:46,300 --> 00:15:48,280
But it also raises questions about responsibility.

191
00:15:48,920 --> 00:15:52,260
If an AI system recommends a dangerous experiment, who is responsible?

192
00:15:53,102 --> 00:15:56,840
If an automated laboratory produces an unexpected result, who verifies it?

193
00:15:57,326 --> 00:16:03,920
If a machine generates a scientific discovery using millions of pieces of data, how to researchers establish that the conclusion is reproducible?

194
00:16:04,750 --> 00:16:09,780
And if a machine contributes substantially to a breakthrough, how should scientific credit be assigned?

195
00:16:10,478 --> 00:16:12,560
These are not distant philosophical questions.

196
00:16:13,326 --> 00:16:19,200
They are practical challenges that researchers, universities, laboratories, and technology companies are already beginning to confront.

197
00:16:19,758 --> 00:16:22,860
The scientific revolution will require more than powerful AI models.

198
00:16:23,662 --> 00:16:30,600
It will require reliable data, transparent experiments, independent verification, strong safety systems, and clear human responsibility.

